keeps: whether
bars: returns true | returns false | \bhasrateatall\b
why: run 16 wrote that a function asks without a mode and returns true where the platform ignores the mode; what the function tells its caller, and why asking that way gives it, was reached only by reading the body and the caller; Kirill: the reader should not have to open the implementation to understand the comment, so a reader holding the block and the signature alone says what the result tells the caller, and the caller's need as its reason. Re-cut 2026-09-29 under §97 to another fact of the class, since the brief's exemplar now carries the original; its earlier figures no longer compare.
labelled: 2026-09-29
--- code
export async function hasRateAtAll(currency: string): Promise<boolean> {
  const quotes = await rateService.quotes({ currency });
  return quotes.length > 0;
}
--- callers
// revaluation/Revaluation.ts
export async function revalue(book: LedgerBook): Promise<void> {
  if (!(await hasRateAtAll(book.currency))) {
    return;
  }

  await revalueEntries(book);
}
--- facts
A rate service missing a currency altogether hangs on the first revaluation that asks for it, whatever date the revaluation names, and the revaluation in `revaluation/Revaluation.ts` runs first.
So this function asks the rate service for the currency without a date, and returns true when any quote comes back.
